## A B S T R A C T
In this work, a molecularly imprinted photoelectrochemical (MIP-PEC) sensor based on a novel PEC composite of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{(NH_{2}{-}M I L{-}}125\mathrm{(Ti){-}T i O_{2})}$ was established for the ultrasensitive and selective detection of oxytetracycline (OTC). This is the first attempt of applying MOFs in the construction of MIP-PEC sensor. The $\mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})\mathrm{-TiO_{2}}$ was synthesized by a simple one-step solvothermal method and modified onto the surface of indium tin oxide (ITO) electrode as the photosensitive layer. Subsequently, molecularly imprinted polymer (MIP) was modified as recognition element by electropolymerization. The $\mathrm{{NH}_{2}}$ - $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti}){-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ showed an enhanced photocurrent response due to stronger light absorption capacity and matched energy band. Furthermore, MIP greatly improved the selectivity and sensitivity of the constructed PEC sensor. The photocurrent response of the MIP-PEC sensor was reduced after OTC recognition because the specific binding of OTC to the imprinted cavities blocked the electron transfer of the electrode. Under optimal experi­ mental conditions, the MIP-PEC sensor exhibited a wide detection range from $0.1{\mathrm{nM}}$ to $10\upmu\mathrm{M}$ with a low limit of detection (LOD) of $60\mathrm{pM}$ , as well as certain reproducibility, stability and good applicability in real samples. The proposed sensor provides ideas for the application of MOFs in the construction of PEC sensors and will offer an alternative method for the detection of other pollutants in the field of food safety.
## 1. Introduction
Oxytetracycline (OTC) has been widely used as a veterinary drug for human therapy, aquaculture and fruit production. Allergic reactions and antibiotic resistance could be caused by the excessive residue of OTC in the animal-derived foods (Lee et al., 2020). At present, common analytical methods widely applied for OTC detection are high perfor­ mance liquid chromatography (HPLC) (Hu et al., 2018; Mohebi et al., 2020; Moumita et al., 2019), liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectroscopy (LC-MS/MS) (Do et al., 2020; Krijt et al., 2017) and so on (Bai et al., 2020; Chen et al., 2020c). However, these methods based on chromatography technique often need expensive instruments, experi­ enced operators and complex sample treatment (Chen et al., 2020a; Ma et al., 2020), which can not meet the demands of on-site rapid detection in food safety analysis. Therefore, it is urgent to develop a simple, sen­ sitive and reliable on-site rapid detection method for OTC.
Photoelectrochemical (PEC) sensors have attracted analytical sci­ entists’ attention worldwide due to the unique advantages of low cost, easy of miniaturization, high sensitivity and rapid turnaround time (Liu et al., 2020; Zheng et al., 2020). Molecularly imprinted photo­ electrochemical (MIP-PEC) sensor using molecular imprinting polymer (MIP) as the sensitive recognition element has the advantages of high sensitivity and selectivity, facile preparation and low cost, providing new design strategies for the construction of PEC sensors with excellent performance (Dashtian et al., 2020; Yang et al., 2019). Up to now, MIP-PEC sensors have been widely explored, and extensively applied in food safety analysis, environmental monitoring and medical testing (Cui et al., 2020; Wang et al., 2020).
Though MIP-based PEC sensor has excellent selectivity, it should be noted that photoactive materials play a crucial role in the construction of PEC sensing platform, which greatly affect the performance of PEC sensors (Ouyang et al., 2020). Due to its stable PEC performance, low cost and nontoxicity, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is the most widely used photoactive material in the construction of PEC sensors (Chen et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2020). However, the fast recombination of photo-generated electron-hole pairs and the narrow optical response of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ make it only applicable to ul­ traviolet light $\left(<387~\mathrm{nm}\right)$ (Jin et al., 2015). Many attempts have been made for better application of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in the PEC sensor, in which compositing $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with other advanced functional materials is an effec­ tive way (Ali et al., 2020; Zhan et al., 2020).
As a class of porous functional materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with controllable pore structure and large specific surface area have been used to couple with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to overcome the limitation of intrinsic performance of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and improve PEC performance of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Tang et al. fabricated a photoanode composed of layered $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ coupled with $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ and MOF which can effectively prolong the light traveling length and expedite the interfacial carrier transport (Tang et al., 2017). MOF-derived hierarchical heterostructure of $\mathrm{Co_{3}C/T i O_{2}}$ with enhanced the light harvesting capability and carrier transportation efficiency was also reported by Tang et al., (2018). Recently, Yang et al. prepared nanorod arrays of core-shell $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/M O F}$ which can facilitate separation of electron-hole pairs and improve charge density at the interface of photoanode and electrolyte (Quiroz et al., 2020). As a robust amino-functionalized titanium (IV) based MOFs, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) not only has special characteristics of MOFs, but also has wonderful pho­ toluminescence properties and photocatalytic activities, which enables semiconducting application under visible-light (Mahmoodi et al., 2019). Moreover, enhanced PEC performances have been achieved when $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) was coupled with other semiconductor to form com­ posite materials (Ao et al., 2018; Dhivya et al., 2019; Yoon et al., 2019), indicating its potential applications in PEC and MIP-PEC sensors.
In this work, an MIP-PEC sensing platform based on a novel PEC composite of $\mathrm{NH_{2}}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125(\mathrm{Ti}){\-}\mathrm{TiO_{2}}$ was constructed for the sensitive and selective detection of OTC. Firstly, the novel PEC composite $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ - $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti}){-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was synthesized using facile one-step solvothermal approach, which was used as the photoelectrical converter of the PEC sensor. MIP recognition element was fabricated on the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti)– $\mathrm{\cdotTiO_{2}}$ by electropolymerizaiton to achieve high selectivity. The experimental results confirmed that the MIP-PEC sensor had low detection limit, excellent selectivity, stability and acceptable reproduc­ ibility. The application results obtained by using the facile MIP-PEC sensor was comparable to the results obtained by using wellestablished HPLC method. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first time to use MOFs-based composite as the photoelectrical converter for the construction of a flexible MIP-PEC sensing platform.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Chemicals and reagents
$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanopowder (anatase, $<25~\mathrm{nm}$ , $99.7\%$ was purchased from Xianfeng Nano Material Technology Co., Ltd. (Jiangsu, China). Tetra­ butyl titanate (TBT), 2-aminoterephthalic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC-NH}_{2})$ , N, Ndimethylformamide (DMF), and methanol were purchased from Sino­ pharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China). OTC, $o$ -phenyl­ enediamine $(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{2})$ (o-PD), aureomycin (AM), ampicillin (AMP), tetracycline (TET), doxycycline (DOXY) were purchased from Aladdin Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Ultrapure water was used throughout the experiment. All chemical reagents were at least analytical grade and used without further purification.
## 2.2. Apparatus
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) were measured on a JEOL JSM7100F scanning electron microscope (Jeol, Japan) to characterize the morphologies of different modified electrode. X-ray diffraction (XRD) spectra were achieved by $\mathrm{D}5000\mathrm{X}$ ray diffractometer (Siemens, Germany) using Cu Kα exciting source $(\lambda\stackrel{\cdot}{=}1.54056\stackrel{\circ}{\mathrm{A}})$ . Ultraviolet visible (UV–vis) diffuse reflectance spectra were acquired using a UV–vis spectrometer (Hitachi, U–3900H). Fluorescence (FL) spectra were recorded on an F97pro fluorescence spectrophotometer (Lengguang Technology Co., Ltd., Shanghai, China). Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra were performed on a Nicolet iS50 (Thermo Scientific, America). All the electrochemical experiments were recorded on a CHI 660D electrochemical analyzer (Chenhua Instrument Co., Ltd., Shanghai, China). The photoelectrochemical experiments were con­ ducted using a PEAC 200A PEC reaction instrument (Tianjin Aida­ hengsheng Science-Technology Development Co., Ltd., Tianjin, China). PEC signals were carried out by CHI660D electrochemical workstation (Chenhua Instrument Co., Ltd., Shanghai, China) with a three-electrode system, using ITO electrode $(10\times45\times1~\mathrm{{mm})}$ (South China Science &Technology Co., Ltd., Zhuhai, China), platinum wire, and saturated $\mathtt{A g/A g C l}$ as working electrode, counter electrode, and reference elec­ trode, respectively. A type of white light source was used as the irradi­ ation source (simulated sunlight irradiation) to provide visible light with the range of $400{-}800~\mathrm{nm}$ . The working surface area of ITO electrodes was $0.5\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ . HPLC system (Agilent Technologies Inc., Santa Clara, CA, USA) was used for HPLC analysis with a variable-wavelength UV de­ tector (Agilent Technologies 1200 series VWD).
## 2.3. Preparation of $N H_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti), $N H_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)–TiO2
$\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) was synthesized according to the reported method (Jin et al., 2015). $1.5\mathrm{mmol}$ of TBT, 3 mmol of $\mathrm{{H_{2}B D C-N H_{2}}}$ , $12.5\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF, and $12.5~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol were mixed. After stirred at room tem­ perature (RT) for $30~\mathrm{min}$ , the mixture was carefully transferred into a Teflon lined autoclave, and was heated at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{h}$ . After cooling to RT, the as-prepared yellow powder product was filtered, and washed thrice with DMF to remove the unreacted organic ligands, and then washed several times with methanol to exchange DMF with methanol. Finally, the obtained yellow power was dried under vacuum at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{h}}$ to give the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti). The $\mathrm{NH_{2}}–\mathrm{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})–\mathrm{TiO_{2}}$ composite was prepared using the method described above but with the addition of 6 mmol $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles into the mixture of TBT, $\mathrm{{H_{2}B D C-N H_{2}}}$ , DMF and methanol.
## 2.4. Construction of the MIP-PEC sensing platform
The construction process of the MIP-PEC sensing platform is illus­ trated in Scheme 1. Firstly, the ITO electrode was cleaned with ethanol by ultrasonic treatment for $5\mathrm{min}$ , and then washed with ultrapure water and dried at RT. The $\mathrm{NH_{2}}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125(\mathrm{Ti}){\-}\mathrm{TiO_{2}}$ suspension $(1\mathrm{\mg\mL}^{-1}).$ ) using ultrapure water as the dispersant was dropped onto the surface of the ITO electrode and dried at RT to obtain $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L{-}}125(\mathrm{Ti})/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}/\mathrm{ITO}$ electrode. Then, the MIP film was fabricated by placing the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL$125(\mathrm{Ti})/\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}/\mathrm{ITO}$ electrode in a acetate buffer solution $\mathrm{(pH}=5.2\AA$ containing $0.05~\mathrm{\mM}$ $o$ -PD and $0.02~\mathrm{\mM}$ OTC using electro­ polymerization via cyclic voltammetry (CV) in the range of $_{0-0.8\mathrm{~V~}}$ at $50\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ for 15 cycles (Zhong et al., 2019). After eluted with $5\%\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ aqueous solution for $5\mathrm{min}$ , the modified electrode was carefully washed with ultrapure water and air-dried, which was denoted as $\mathrm{{MIP/NH_{2}}}.$ ­ MIL-125(Ti)–TiO2/ITO electrode. Thus, sensing platform of the MIP-PEC sensor was obtained and used for the following experiments. As a control, the non-MIP (NIP)/ $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti)–TiO2/ITO electrode was prepared in the same steps except adding OTC.
## 2.5. Photoelectrochemical measurement
The $\mathrm{{MIP/NH_{2}}}$ -MIL-125(Ti)–TiO2/ITO electrode was incubated in OTC solution for $5\mathrm{{min}}$ . Then, the electrode was taken out and washed with ultrapure water to eliminate non-specifically adsorbed substances. Photocurrent responses were measured at a constant potential of $0\mathrm{v}$ in a 0.1 M $\mathrm{Na_{2}S O_{4}}$ supporting electrolyte solution with a three-electrode system illuminated by $400{-}800\ \mathrm{nm}$ light (Wang et al., 2018). The light was switched on $30~\mathsf{s}$ and off 30 s in turn.
## 2.6. Sample treatment and HPLC validation
The practical tap water sample was collected from our laboratory in Shanxi University. The milk and chicken samples were purchased from a local market. The spiking and recovery experiments were carried out to assess the applicability of the proposed MIP-PEC sensor. Certain vol­ umes of OTC standard solution were spiked into the solid and liquid samples at three levels: 1.0, 5.0 and $10.0\ \mathrm{nM}$ or nmol $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ before any pretreatment.
## 2.6.1. Water sample treatment
For MIP-PEC detection, the tap water sample was simply filtered by an ordinary filter paper to remove the suspended matters, and then passed through $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ nylon filter membrane.
For HPLC detection, $10\mathrm{mL}$ water sample was extracted with 6 mL nhexane for three times. Then, the extract was combined and concen­ trated to dryness. Finally, the residue was diluted with $1\mathrm{mL}$ (methanol: ultrapure water $=1{:}9$ ), and then passed through $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ nylon filter membrane for detection.
## 2.6.2. Milk and chicken sample treatment
For MIP-PEC detection, $2\mathrm{mL}$ or $_{2g}$ of sample was vortex mixed with 8 mL 0.1 M $\mathbf{Na}_{2}$ EDTA-Mcllvaine $\mathrm{(pH}=4\AA.$ ) to deposit protein and fat and dissolve organic substances in the matrix. The mixture was then centrifuged at $4500\mathrm{rpm}$ for $15\mathrm{min}$ twice to separate the deposition for detection.
For HPLC analysis, the supernatants were concentrated to dryness to evaporate the solvent. Finally, the residue was diluted with $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ (methanol: ultrapure water $=1{:}9.$ ), then filtrated with $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ nylon filter membrane.
The HPLC measurement was performed with $\mathbf{C}_{18}$ column $(200\mathrm{mm}\times$ $4.6\mathrm{mm}\dot{}$ ) using 0.01 M oxalic acid/acetonitrile/methanol (v:v:v, 7:2:1) as the mobile phase with a flow rate of $1\mathrm{mL}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . Detection wavelength was set as $355\mathrm{nm}$ and the column temperature was $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterizations of $N H_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)–TiO2 and MIP-PEC sensing platform
The typical SEM images of $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/I T O}$ (A), $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)/ITO (B) and $\mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})\mathrm{-TiO_{2}}/\mathrm{ITO}$ (C) were showed in Fig. 1. It was observed that $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ exhibited as uniform spherical particles; whereas $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) exhibited as nanosheets with relatively regular shape, which was consistent with the reported literature (Jin et al., 2015). The SEM image of $\mathrm{{NH}_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti)–TiO2 showed that the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ were dispersed on the surface of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) and formed a three-dimension structure, which may be favorable for the trans­ portation of electronics and may provide more imprinting cavities for subsequent MIP film modification (Wang et al., 2018). As showed in Fig. 1D, MIP film was uniformly modified on the surface of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti)– $\mathrm{\cdotTiO}_{2}$ , indicating that the sensing platform of MIP-PEC sensor had been successfully constructed.
Meanwhile, the structural properties of $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/I T O}$ (a), $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti)/ITO (b), $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L{-}}125(\mathrm{Ti}){-}\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}/\mathrm{ITO}$ (c) were characterized by XRD technique (Fig. S1). It was obvious that $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L{-}}125(\mathrm{Ti}){-}\mathrm{TiO_{2}}$ had a diffraction peak at $9^{\circ}$ , which was consistent with the diffraction peak of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti). Other peaks were basically consistent with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , confirming the successful preparation of $\mathrm{{NH}_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti)– $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ com­ posite. In addition, the characteristic diffraction peaks of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti)– ${\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}}/\mathrm{TTO}$ were still well-retained after further MIP modification (curve d), indicating that the modification of MIP film did not vary the crystalline structure of $\mathrm{NH_{2}}–\mathrm{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})–\mathrm{TiO_{2}}$ .
UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra and FL spectra were employed to evaluate the optical property of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})–\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . As showed in Fig. 2A, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ can absorb the light in the wavelength from 200 to $414\mathrm{nm}$ and the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) showed absorption edge below $500\mathrm{nm}$ . After $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) was combined with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , the $\mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})\mathrm{-TiO_{2}}$ composite exhibited significantly increased visible-light absorption in­ tensity and a broaden absorption range. A novel impurity level in the forbidden band of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ may be produced with the integrating of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - MIL-125(Ti), thereby reducing the band gap and increasing the ab­ sorption in visible light. The integration of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) into the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ improved the optical absorption performance of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , which was beneficial to enhance PEC response. Fluorescence measurement can be used to study the photo-excited charge carrier transfer and recombina­ tion process of semiconductor materials. It is known that high FL in­ tensity indicates a high recombination rate and a weak photocurrent response. The FL spectra of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})\mathrm{-TiO_{2}}$ using the excitation wavelength of ${320}\mathrm{nm}$ were recorded in Fig. 2B. The emission peak of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ appeared at $375\ \mathrm{nm}$ , while the $\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)– $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ composite had no emission peak at $375\mathrm{nm}$ . Interestingly, the emission peak of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti}){-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was similar to $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) in Fig. 2C, but the emission intensity decreased. It was further indicated that $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)– $\mathrm{\cdotTiO_{2}}$ had an excellent separation rate of photogenerated electron-hole pairs compared with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti). In addition, the alone emission peak of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) appeared at $420\mathrm{nm}$ , while the emission peak of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)– $\mathrm{\cdotTiO_{2}}$ decreased and appeared at $440\ \mathrm{nm}$ with a red shift of fluorescence which was consistent with Fig. 2F. The above results demonstrated that $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL$125(\mathrm{Ti}){-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ composite was successfully prepared and had potential photoelectrochemical performance. Furthermore, the FL spectra of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti}){-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ using different preparation methods were showed in Fig. 2D, the emission peak of the composite prepared using one-step solvothermal method was lower than that of composite prepared by simple physical mixing. It indicated that $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)– $\mathrm{\cdotTiO_{2}}$ using one-step solvothermal method had an excellent separation rate of electron-hole pairs and photocurrent performance.
FT-IR spectra of $\mathrm{MIP/NH_{2}\mathrm{-}M I L\mathrm{-}125(T i)\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}/I T O}$ electrode before template elution and $\mathrm{MIP/NH_{2}}\mathrm{-MIL\mathrm{-}}125(\mathrm{Ti})\mathrm{-TiO_{2}}/\mathrm{ITO}$ electrode after template elution were recorded to characterize the MIP film. As showed in Fig. S1(B), the characteristic peaks $1440~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1640~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and 3407 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ of $o$ -PD can be found on the curves, which was consistent with the reported literature (Bai et al., 2020). The peak value of $1440~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $3407~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were assigned to the carbon-carbon double bond (C–C) stretching vibration on the benzene ring, and characteristic vibration of the nitrogen-hydrogen (N–H) bond on the amino group, respectively. The appearance of these characteristic peaks demonstrated the success of $o$ -PD electropolymerization. Furthermore, the peak around 3407 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ was consisted of the stretching vibration absorption peak of hy­ droxyl (O–H) and the stretching vibration absorption peak of N–H (Xi et al., 2020). The peak width increased when hydrogen bonds were formed between $o$ -PD and OTC, and the peak width decreased after elution, indicating that template was removed from the MIP film.
Furthermore, the CV curves of MIP and NIP electropolymerization were compared in Fig. S3. The CV curves were suppressed with an increasing number of CV scans, which was attributed to the formation of non-conductive $o$ -PD film. No significant difference was observed be­ tween the CV curves obtained in presence (A) and in absence of OTC (B) during the $o$ -PD polymerization. These results demonstrated that OTC did not show any electroactivity on the ITO electrode over the scanned potential window.
## 3.2. Calculation of HOMO/LUMO or VB/CB energy levels of materials
In order to further explore the photoelectrochemical performance of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)– $\mathrm{\cdotTiO_{2}}$ composite by theory, the energy band of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) was calculated. Mostly, the optical band gap of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) were calculated by employing the following equations (Mao et al., 2019a): $\mathrm{E}_{g}=1240/\lambda_{o n s e t}$ , $\operatorname{E}_{g}$ is optical band gap, $\lambda_{o n s e t}$ is first exciton absorption peak. As showed in Fig. 2A, $\lambda_{o n s e t}$ of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})$ were determined as $414~\mathrm{nm}$ and $500~\mathrm{nm}$ , corre­ sponding to $\mathbf{E}_{g}$ of $3.00\mathrm{eV}$ and $2.48\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. The band diagrams of conduction band (CB) and valence band (VB) were obtained by cal­ culations, which based on their bulk electron affinity (EA) and ioniza­ tion potential (IP) by using the following equations (Mao et al., 2019b): $\mathrm{IP}=-\left(4.80-{\mathrm E}_{1/2}{}^{F c/F c^{+}}+{\mathrm E}_{o x}\right)$ , $\operatorname{EA}=\operatorname{IP}+\operatorname{E}_{g}$ . Similarly, the energy levels of lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) and highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) of composites were deduced by the equations: $\mathbf{E}_{H O M O}=\mathrm{~-~}\left(4.80-{\mathrm{~}}\mathbf{E}_{1/2}{}^{F c/F c^{+}}+{\mathrm{~}}\mathbf{E}_{o x}\right)$ , $\mathrm{E}_{L U M O}=\mathrm{IP}+\mathrm{E}_{H U M O}$ $\begin{array}{r l}{\lefteqn{(\mathrm{E}_{1/2}^{}{F c/F c^{+}}}}&{{}}\end{array}$ is the formal potential of $F c/F c^{+}$ , $\mathbf{E}_{o x}$ is the oxidation initiation potential) under the premise that the energy level of $F c/F c^{+}$ was $4.8\:\mathrm{eV}$ below the vacuum level (Pommerehne et al., 1995). As can be seen from Fig. 2E, the $\mathbf{E}_{o x}$ of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{{NH}_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) were disclosed as $2.55\mathrm{V}$ and $2.60~\mathrm{V}.$ . From Fig. 2E inset, $\mathbf{E}_{1/2}F c/F c^{+}$ was measured as $+0.37\mathrm{~V~}$ Using the equations, the CB/VB or LUMO/HOMO of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) were calculated as $-3.98\mathrm{\eV/-6.98\mathrm{\eV}}$ and $-4.55$ $\mathrm{eV/-}7.03\mathrm{eV}$ respectively, and the results were listed in Table S1. It was indicated the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{{NH}_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) had matched energy band to facilitate separation of electron-hole pairs, leading to good photo­ electrochemical performance.
## 3.3. Electrochemical behaviors of different modified electrodes
The electrochemical behaviors of different modified electrodes were investigated by CV (Fig. 3A) and electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) (Fig. 3B). Compared with bare ITO electrode (curve a), the redox peak current of $[\mathrm{Fe(CN)}_{6}]^{3-/4-}$ was reduced after the modification of $\mathrm{NH_{2}}–\mathrm{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})–\mathrm{TiO_{2}}$ on ITO electrode (curve b). When the MIP film was modified on the surface of $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L{-}}125(\mathrm{Ti}){-}\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}/\mathrm{ITO}$ electrode (curve c), the redox peak current was further reduced. This phenomenon can be attributed to the fact that the modification of nonconductive MIP film on the electrode surface hindered the electron transfer of the probe. The redox peak current of the MIP/ $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti)–TiO2/ITO elec­ trode recovered after template removal (curve d), because a series of imprinted cavities were emerged after the template elution process. The imprinted cavities left in the MIP film can provide pathways between the probe and the electrode surface. Subsequent incubation of the MIP $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ - $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti}){-}\mathrm{Ti}0_{2}/\mathrm{ITO}$ electrode in the OTC solution caused decrease of the redox peak current (curve e) due to the reoccupation of the imprinted cavities by template molecules.
EIS is an effective method for the evaluation of sensor construction process by monitoring the changes of charge transfer resistance on the electrode interface. As showed in Fig. 3B, the resistance of ITO electrode was small (curve a), indicating that ITO electrode had excellent elec­ trochemical performance. However, the resistance of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)– $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/I T O}$ electrode was slightly increased (curve b), indicating no great impact on the electrical conductivity of the electrode after the modifi­ cation of $\mathrm{NH_{2}}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125(\mathrm{Ti})/\mathrm{TiO_{2}},$ which also laid good foundation for the subsequent construction of the MIP-PEC sensing platform. When the MIP film was electropolymerized on the modified electrode (curve c), the resistance of electrode increased obviously due to the high density of the nonconductive MIP film. The resistance decreased rapidly after the template removal, (curve d), indicating that imprinted cavities were formed as electron transfer channels to accelerate electron transfer. There was a slight increase of resistance after incubation in the OTC solution due to the reoccupation of the imprinted cavities by targets (curve e). These results were in consistent with that of CV, indicating that the MIP-PEC sensing platform had been established successfully, which can recognize the target molecules specifically.
## 3.4. PEC responses of the modified electrodes
The photoelectric conversion properties of different modified ITO electrode were evaluated under PEC measurement equipped with light source. The photocurrent responses of ITO, $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/I T O}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti)/ITO, $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L{-}}125\mathrm{(Ti)}{-}\mathrm{TiO_{2}}/\mathrm{ITO}$ were recorded in Fig. 3C. No photocurrent response was observed on bare ITO electrode (curve a), while the $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/I T O}$ electrode showed low photocurrent response due to the narrow band gap of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (curve b). When $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti) was modified onto the electrode (curve c), the photocurrent response was slightly improved compared with $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/I T O}$ electrode. Greatly enhanced photocurrent was obtained on $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)–TiO2/ITO electrode (curve d). It could be attributed to stronger light absorption capacity and the matched energy band of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})–\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ composite. As depicted in Scheme 1, electrons on the VB absorbed photons and then transferred to the CB to form electron-hole pairs when the photoelectric material was illuminated. Since the CB potential energy of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125 (Ti) $(-4.55\mathrm{eV})$ lied more negative than the CB potential energy of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\left(-3.98\mathrm{eV}\right)$ , photo-generated electrons coming from $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ could be easily injected into the CB of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) and flow to the external circuit through the conductive ITO electrode. Simultaneously, the holes on the VB of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–125(\mathrm{Ti})$ $\left(-7.03\ \mathrm{eV}\right)$ could migrate to the VB of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\left(-6.98\mathrm{eV}\right)$ , suppressing the electron-hole pair recombination effectively. The positive holes were driven to the ITO electrode surface and got captured by water $(\mathrm{h^{+}}+\mathrm{H^{+}}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\to2\mathrm{OH}\cdot\mathrm{\Omega})$ . Furthermore, the electrons flowed to the external circuit would cause the reduction of the oxidized form of water at the counter electrode (Pt) $({\bf e}^{-}+{\bf O}{\bf H}\cdot+{\bf H}^{+}\rightarrow{\bf H}_{2}{\bf O})$ . This manner of the circuit demonstrated a self-powered detection property of the PEC system with no electron or hole scavengers. Owing to the excellent photoelectric conversion performance, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)– $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ composite could be utilized as an ideal PEC material to combine with MIP for high-performance sensing application.
As showed in Fig. 3D, photocurrent responses of different modified ITO electrodes during the construction process of sensing platform were recorded. The MIP $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)–TiO2/ITO electrode before tem­ plate elution had a weak photocurrent response (curve c), which was not much different from the bare ITO electrode (curve a). After the template removal, the photocurrent response was significantly promoted (curve d), indicating that imprinted cavities of OTC were emerged, which were acting as the electron transfer pathways. After incubation of OTC, the photocurrent response was reduced owing to the blocked imprinted cavities (curve e). Due to the specific recognition capacity of MIP, OTC was successfully entrapped onto the surface of MIP $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti)– $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/I T O}$ electrode, hindering the electron transfer greatly and resulting in the recombination of electron-hole pairs. Thus, noteworthy decrease of photocurrent was observed. The imprinting effect of the proposed MIP-PEC sensing platform was further investigated by the comparison of the photocurrent responses of the $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/I T O}$ , $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L{-}}125(\mathrm{Ti}){-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ ITO, NIP $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)–TiO2/ITO and MIP $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti)– $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{TTO}$ before and after incubation in $1.0\mathrm{nM}$ OTC solution (Fig. S2). The largest photocurrent change was obtained on the MIP $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ -MIL-125 (Ti $){-}\operatorname{TiO}_{2}/\operatorname{TTO}$ , proving strong specific recognition capacity of MIP film.
## 3.5. Optimization of experimental conditions
The photocurrent responses of the MIP-PEC sensor changing with elution time was showed in Fig. 3E. The photocurrent remained almost unchanged after $5\mathrm{min}$ of elution, indicating that OTC had almost been removed from the MIP film. Thus, $5~\mathrm{min}$ was selected as the optimal elution time. Fig. 3F showed the photocurrent responses of the MIP-PEC sensor changing with different incubation time. As the incubation time increased, the photocurrent response apparently decreased and remained almost stable after $5~\mathrm{min}$ of incubation, indicating that the adsorption of template molecules by MIP film had reached equilibrium. Thus, $5\mathrm{min}$ was selected as the optimal incubation time.
Experimental conditions including pH value of OTC solution for in­ cubation and incubation temperature were optimized. As showed in Fig. S4, $\mathrm{pH}7.0$ was selected as the optimal pH of OTC solution for in­ cubation and the detection of OTC using MIP-PEC sensor could be conducted at RT.
## 3.6. Analytical performance of the MIP-PEC sensor
Analytical performance of the MIP-PEC sensor was investigated by recording the corresponding photocurrents of OTC solutions with different concentrations. As showed in Fig. 4A, the photocurrent response of the MIP-PEC sensor gradually decreased along with the in­ crease of OTC concentrations. The photocurrent change $(\triangle\mathrm{I})$ was lin­ early proportional to the logarithm of the OTC concentration over the range of $0.1\ \mathrm{nM-}10\ \upmu\mathrm{M}$ (Fig. 4B). The linear regression equations was $\triangle{\mathrm{I}}\left({\upmu{\sf A}}\right)=0.0628$ logC $\mathrm{(mM)}+0.529$ , with a correlation coefficient $(\mathbb{R}^{2})$ of 0.993. Besides, the limit of detection (LOD, $s/\Nu=3.$ ) was esti­ mated to be $60\mathrm{pM}$ . The proposed MIP-PEC sensor showed a wider linear range with a lower LOD than those of previously reported OTC detected methods (Table 1).
## 3.7. Selectivity, anti-interference ability, reproducibility and stability of the MIP-PEC sensor
The selectivity of the MIP-PEC sensor was studied by measuring its photocurrent towards $1.0\mathrm{nM}$ of OTC and $1.0\mathrm{nM}$ of its structural analogs (TET, AM, AMP, DOX). The results are showed in Fig. 4C and the chemical structures of TET, AM, AMP and DOXY are displayed in Scheme S1. The $\triangle\mathrm{I}$ of $\mathrm{MIP/NH_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}M I L\mathrm{\cdot}125(T i)\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}/I T O}$ electrode to­ wards OTC was much higher than that of its analogs, indicating that the proposed MIP-PEC sensor had good selectivity. Imprinting factors (IFs) were calculated using the following equation:
$$
\mathrm{IF}{=}\frac{\triangle\mathrm{I_{(MIP)}}}{\triangle\mathrm{I_{(NIP)}}}
$$
As can be seen from Fig. 4C, the IFs of OTC, TET, AM, AMP, DOXY were 7.3, 1.05, 1.25, 1.125 and 1.09, respectively. These results demonstrated that the MIP-PEC sensor had specific recognition for OTC. Meanwhile, the anti-interference ability of proposed MIP-PEC sensor was evaluated by recording photocurrent of detecting OTC alone, and the mixture of OTC and interferences. As can be seen in Fig. 4D, inter­ fering substances with ten times of the OTC concentration cannot interfere with OTC detection, revealing good anti-interference ability of the MIP-PEC sensing platform. Evidently, the good selectivity and antiinterference ability of the proposed MIP-PEC sensor were originated from the imprinting effect of MIP, endowing the MIP-PEC sensor with good application performance.
Generally, the stability of the sensor is important for its practical application. As showed in Fig. 4E, the photocurrent responses remained relatively stable after 20 on/off irradiation cycles for $400\ s.$ And the photocurrent responses only decreased less than $1.9\%$ after the sensing platform was stored at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for one month, indicating that the fabricated MIP-PEC sensor had good stability and long-term stability. Moreover, six independently prepared $\mathbf{MIP/NH_{2}}$ -MIL-125(Ti)– $.\mathrm{TiO_{2}/I T O}$ electrodes were investigated to evaluate the reproducibility of prepared MIP-PEC sensor. As showed in Fig. 4F, a relative standard deviation (RSD) for detection of $1.0\mathrm{nM}$ OTC was $2.0\%$ , indicating that the proposed sensor had excellent reproducibility.
## 3.8. Real sample analysis
The application performance of the proposed sensor was estimated using spiking and recovery method. As showed in Table S2, recovery rates of the MIP-PEC sensor ranged from $95.2\%$ to $101.6\%$ with the RSD of $1.1{-}2.3\%$ . The results using HPLC method were in accordance with the results obtained by the MIP-PEC sensor, revealing that the estab­ lished MIP-PEC sensor exhibited good accuracy and application per­ formance. More importantly, compared with different methods used for OTC detection, the analysis progress by the present MIP-PEC sensor owned better characteristics, such as low-cost, rapid speed and simple operation.
## 4. Conclusions
In this work, a novel MIP-PEC sensing platform based on the com­ posite $\mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})\mathrm{-TiO_{2}}$ was prepared for ultrasensitive and se­ lective detection of OTC. The $\mathrm{NH_{2}}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125(\mathrm{Ti}){\-}\mathrm{TiO_{2}}$ composite synthesized by simple one-step solvothermal method showed enhanced photocurrent performance and its mechanism had been investigated in detail. The proposed MIP-PEC sensing platform showed excellent analytical performance with remarkable selectivity, anti-interference ability, reproducibility and stability, holding great promise for the ap­ plications in the field of food safety and environment monitoring. Nevertheless, the linear range in this work was still unsatisfying compared to previously reported OTC detected methods (Table 1). And the electrode preparation procedure is laborious and time-consuming. Thus, future work should focus on the improvement of superperformance photoactive materials and the portable sensing platform. The first attempt of applying MOFs in the field of MIP-PEC sensor may provide a new insight into the design and application of the MOFs-based MIP-PEC sensor.
